4.1. A Need for Recognition
i. 	This is very clearly not the community for one looking for influence, at least 
numbers-wise. What is earned by rising up ranks and recognition is power in this 
community; hunters manage groups while myths manage their group of obsessors. 
People actually want this power despite the lack of people, perhaps for what they can get 
away with, but for most reasonable people, this power is what fulfills them; their passion 
in the community is the people themselves, and serving them in some way is most 
virtuous. The grasping for this power, among the younger crowd more commonly, can be 
misguided; their level does not match the requirement for the rank or power they wish 
to receive. For myths, because this is less observable in hunters, maybe their rank was 
based on quality, and their swift want for acknowledgment led them to overlook the 
creative integrity or the effort they had for their game. When they do not receive the 
rank they had wished for, they do not learn to put in more effort, they do not connect 
their art to the rank; they end up with hate and a lack of will to improve the quality of 
their work. It drives the want to take shortcuts, and it drives the will to get places no 
matter how qualified, even if it means cheating, scamming, or using people without 
proper credit. It is this lack of self-consciousness that pervades the people in this 
community, only tearing it down further, while remaining a more hidden issue.

ii. 	The collapse of the community led to a situation where thousands were left still 
engaged and passionate about the community, but hating every moment considering 
where it was at, those in power, the optics of the community, and the overall creative 
abilities of the creators. Despite the negatives, people remain for the few positives of 
socialization and community structure; they remain for some feeling of obligation, or for 
the creators, they do not depend on that social affirmation that people away from the 
community generally need. This subconscious knowledge of the community’s failures 
puts less weight on the art they are crafting; it feels like there is no point at all in 
creating, but they still do. It can push people in two radical directions: either never 
pushing for recognition, notably when the myth is of very high quality, or making them 
push a lot harder for recognition, even when lacking the quality or character needed. If 
we keep holding on to this miserable past, there is no sight for the future; people don’t 
know how they should represent themselves socially, or for groups.

