4.2. Cheap Art

i. 	The myth community carries many different types of people; demographics range 
widely in terms of skill, time in the community, and type of engagement with the 
community. Myths, as one of if not the most important parts of the community, are a 
creative endeavor, and one which requires time and effort to do well as. With this hefty 
skill requirement, needing a solid game, along with the attention brought to myths, many 
covet the idea of being a myth without the capability to create one. This is not too 
dissimilar to other art communities with skill involved; in every single one, people will 
try to make easy progress in their chosen skill. It is natural, and very notably common in 
the myth community. What contributes to this grasping for skill is, unsurprisingly, a lack 
of maturity and experience within the community or Roblox, along with the lack of will to 
commit time to creation. The myth community has struggled since its popularization, 
constantly trying to deal with those willing to do anything to be a myth, no matter how 
creatively bankrupt.


ii. 	Roblox allows a lot of things for creators; it is a platform for games after all, and 
an unbelievable amount of things make games. With that comes Roblox’s market, or 
toolbox, where creators can publish what they’ve created, with some hindrances. This 
market, and Studio’s toolbox which accesses this market, pushes tons of pre-made 
models ranging from radios, scripts, and trees to skies, lighting, and so on. The models in 
themselves are not inherently creatively bankrupt, but are the opposite. Sharing your 
creations on this market can be rewarding in some small way; the problem lies with the 
myths that use them. Free models have been a topic within the community for ages, 
questions of what truly matters in a myth, the creative ability, modeling ability, scripting 
ability; does it matter for those simply seeking a story or puzzle? Well, to me, a myth is a 
combination of aesthetics, gameplay, and lore, so you must avoid compromising even 
one of those. Free models also denote someone’s experience in making games, and 
foreshadow the quality of the game going forward, but beyond the perception of those 
who use free models, there is a creative harm that it does. 

