iii.	Free models harm both the creator and the observer; the creator can not indulge 
in the fulfillment creation brings, the creator themselves had no part in the design of the 
model, and free models are extremely difficult to make a cohesive game with. Free 
models represent a shortcut, cheap creativity blatant to any observer. People believe 
that free models are a place to start for builders or scripters, but there is no building or 
scripting involved; those engaging in free models rarely change the details of the models 
in any transformative way; those learning from free models have a lot else they could 
learn from. Using a few free models in an otherwise well-crafted game can be very 
irritating as well; there is an expectation that you can maintain creative integrity. You 
could bother with the rest of it, but not this small bit? Do you care about the art of it, or 
are you just here to waste time? Their joy comes from easy completion and recognition 
more than the journey of creation itself. An observer who wishes to see a story or puzzle 
may be stopped in their tracks, seeing some ill-crafted puzzle, or having artistic details of 
a story hindered by the use of free models that stand out like a sore thumb. Free models 
abandon the art of creation; your art can’t provide what someone with full control over 
the slightest details can convey. The smallest details matter, and you can learn to build 
your skills, just have some understanding that shortcuts are useless.

iv. 	A less developed idea I’ve formed is the connection between free models and AI. 
Both are found by a prompt or search; neither is made by the person using it, and both 
use the creations of other people. While AI has entire programs for its specific use for a 
wide variety of things, much more than games or Roblox, free models have a specific 
part of Roblox, and can be found with a search rather than a prompt. While sharing a 
cheap access point, they are composed of the creations of others; free models are directly 
created by people, and can be associated with at least one person, while AI is an 
amalgamation of millions of artists into one piece. There is no creation associated with 
AI, while free models have artistry involved, but despite this difference, what matters is 
their use by creators, both slapped into places the creator could’ve formed with their 
own creativity, both used to avoid the effort required to build a skill. 

