4.3. A Black Market of Sorts

i. 	What the myth community has generally avoided is the factor of money in the 
workings of myths. We must first recognize myths as art; then, to note that the myths 
themselves bear the freedom to choose what happens with their art. When you rise into 
higher connections with others in the community, as a hunter or a myth, it can reveal 
some of its workings; how it happens behind the scenes, where the accounts and games 
really come from. One of these workings has hit me recently as an affront to the art we 
wish to form; the market for myths, an exchange of money for the creative rights to the 
art. To be fair on this, it is of course the choice of the myth to sell their art for others to 
portray. One type is the sale of an already notable myth, done out of the felt need to 
continue it beyond what the original creator could bear. This is common, done out of felt 
obligation, and is even more common among true games; most notably, the sale of 
Minecraft to Microsoft in 2011. The sale of a game generally means a turn of creative 
identity; the inheritors of the art are incapable of continuing from the creative vision of 
the original creator, and the choices they make are criticized more. For myths, this 
passing of a myth from one to another is held secret (the problem), and is common 
among the most popular myths, where their creative obligations can not be upheld. 
Although it is common, this continuance is harming the art itself, as what had gotten the 
myth to the heights it had was the original creative direction, and the original direction 
can be hindered as well by such a change. Another type of this selling of art is as 
pre-packaged, pre-built accounts and games, all you have to do is act. This is the most 
hideous form of creative cheapness; there is no journey in this skill-building needed for 
the art, and the art is soulless in itself as well when there is no creativity from the person 
portraying the myth. The passion of creation is not shown; the directed touches needed 
for the quality of a myth are ignored for monetary gain, and inauthenticity is easy to see. 

ii. 	The market is not in any public place; the offers go out to the largest myths of the 
time to buy them out for a varying price, or people contact someone who is directly 
selling pre-built myths. The question is why; the community is tiny, there is no 
significant profit, and it is easiest just to leave it for greener pastures. There is also a 
stigma; of course, you have to keep it as secret as possible. This all works against the 
selling and buying of myths, and yet it continues relative to the size of the community. 
The loss of personally crafted art is a horrid thought; it is best reasoned by the want to 
continue the art beyond the extent of your passion for it, or the need for the easiest form 
of artistic progress, the quickest shortcut to success among peers; they may do it 
because power is easier to gain in this community than in others as an artist. 

iii. 	With any community rises structures of people; the myth community is no 
stranger to this. Of course, groups define it, as shards of the community form structure 
in even the smallest Roblox groups or Discords. The market of myths arose in and 
because of a subsect common in the community, secretive communications, hidden 
chats, and groups where a variety of ideas, prerogatives, or exchanges can form. Because 
the community is comprised of friend groups, it is a shattered community; those in 
friend groups only share their knowledge and ideas with each other. With this 
community of hundreds of hidden groups, nothing can form; no movement, no progress, 
and no overall change can occur. The largest or most important people in the community 
can form groups like these, a few to several people within, whether they control a group, 
a myth, or some movement. Though this is a feeling that happens in any community 
without solid leadership, many can feel like some hidden group of people is controlling 
either most myths or most hunting groups, controlling the ideology or topics of 
discussion in the community. It’s obvious, though, that this community would struggle 
even to form such a powerful group of people to control it. This feeling arises when the 
people in power of hunting groups hold a barrier of reasonable conversation between 
ranks, and when ranking up seems like an impossible task. It can also rise when people 
rise in the community, only to feel empty and without peers; that assurance that there 
are still people above you can be formed out of that wish for there to be more. The 
constant dramas of people in power can also leave a feeling of a vacuum of skill or power; 
the community feels as if it were supposed to fall apart, upheld by some hidden group.
